<BaseLayout title="Manifesto — Axiom Atelier">
  <section class="manifesto">
    <h1>On Essential Subtraction</h1>

    <p>
  <strong>The world does not need more objects. It needs more intentionality.</strong>
</p>

<p>
  At Axiom Atelier, I do not add decoration until an object appears finished. I
  begin with material—with grain, weight, resistance, and possibility—and remove
  everything that conceals its strongest expression.
</p>

<p>
  Every unnecessary line is a distraction.<br />
  Every arbitrary gesture weakens the whole.<br />
  Every decision must earn its place.
</p>

<p><strong>This is Essential Subtraction.</strong></p>

<p>
  It is not reduction for the sake of simplicity. It is the pursuit of what is
  true: the form that remains when excess, imitation, and indecision have been
  stripped away. What survives should feel inevitable—as though the object could
  never have become anything else.
</p>

<p>
  This is the foundation of Latent Form Theory: the conviction that an inherent
  order lies waiting within material, function, and circumstance. My work is a
  dialogue between the organic history of the wood and a modern vision of order.
  My task is not to impose form, but to recognize what is already present and
  remove whatever stands in its way.
</p>

<h2>The Single Hand Standard</h2>

<p>
  <strong>
    True luxury begins with an unbroken chain of custody between artisan and
    artifact.
  </strong>
</p>

<p>
  I personally select every board and follow its grain toward its most
  architectural expression. From the first cut to the final hand-rubbed finish,
  every object that bears the Axiom name remains under the care of one hand and
  the judgment of one eye.
</p>

<p>
  No delegation.<br />
  No production line.<br />
  No shortcuts disguised as efficiency.
</p>

<p>
  Because one maker carries each piece from raw material to final surface, the
  work is naturally scarce. This is not a strategy. It is the necessary
  consequence of making without compromise.
</p>

<p>
  In a world crowded with visual noise, an Axiom object is a point of stillness:
  sculptural utility shaped by disciplined geometry, material intelligence, and
  human presence.
</p>

<p>
  This is not restraint without passion.
</p>

<p>
  <strong>It is passion disciplined by purpose.</strong>
</p>

<p>
  Nothing is added merely to impress.<br />
  Nothing remains without reason.
</p>

<p>
  What leaves the workshop is not simply finished.
</p>

<p><strong>It has been found.</strong></p>

<p>
  <em>— Jason Deters, Founder &amp; Artisan</em>
</p>

<p>
  <a
    href="https://latentformtheory.com/"
    target="_blank"
    rel="noopener noreferrer"
